WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.327
This example updates city through a simple active-patient view.

00:00:04.327 --> 00:00:07.032
RETURNING shows the changed row.

00:00:07.032 --> 00:00:13.637
The transaction then rolls back, so the demonstration does not permanently modify the teaching database.

00:00:13.637 --> 00:00:17.602
This is a good pattern when learning or testing write behavior.

00:00:17.602 --> 00:00:23.167
A standard view stores a query definition, not a separate copy of the rows.

00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:30.307
Treat the selected columns, names, types, and grain as a contract with downstream users.

00:00:30.307 --> 00:00:37.036
Changing that contract can break dashboards or applications even if the underlying query still runs.
